  "implementation": "Use clamped unit-domain spans and the existing bounded curvature sampler to obtain point/curvature/tangent stations. Subdivide when two sampled source tangents turn beyond 90 degrees or a tangent turns more than 60 degrees from the endpoint chord, with 64*machine-epsilon arithmetic slack. Include limiting endpoint directions from the first/last noncoincident control points, honoring rational weight signs; actual zero-speed endpoint samples still remain omitted. Reuse accepted jets as the displayed and extracted comb. Constant spans stop, nonconstant coincident endpoints split, output stations remain bounded by 200000 and tree depth by 32. Existing native builds continue to reuse usd-brep trimming. Density, cache, source-domain tags, one-sided jumps, singular gaps, scale and seam policies remain.",
  "inference": "Ten new angle-family cases test the tangent-to-chord boundary around 60 degrees, unequal endpoint edge lengths and 100-degree endpoint turns. An endpoint-only hypothesis matches those cases and the original three failures, but new interior-wiggle and randomized curves disprove it. Interior sampled tangent checks reproduce 16 of the 18 new stress cases. Two weighted curves still use a denser native grid. The implemented sampler is an independently written source-only display heuristic, not a recovered proprietary algorithm or a continuous angular/curvature certificate.",
